WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.770
File system and content analysis starts with the structure of the media.

2
00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:17.829
Directory layout, file names, extensions, file sizes, timestamps, hidden files, system files, thumbnails, archives, scripts, executables, documents, and images all contribute to the picture.

3
00:00:17.829 --> 00:00:20.175
The point is not to list everything.

4
00:00:20.175 --> 00:00:27.604
It is to identify artifacts relevant to the investigative question and preserve enough context to explain them.

5
00:00:27.604 --> 00:00:32.453
File names and extensions can be useful, but they are not proof of content.

6
00:00:32.453 --> 00:00:38.161
Extensions can be changed, misleading, absent, or inconsistent with the file header.

7
00:00:38.161 --> 00:00:40.898
A document may contain embedded objects.

8
00:00:40.898 --> 00:00:43.635
An archive may contain many nested files.

9
00:00:43.635 --> 00:00:49.579
A shortcut file or metadata file may point to a location or target that is no longer present.

10
00:00:49.579 --> 00:00:54.349
Content analysis should verify what a file is, not only what it is called.

11
00:00:54.349 --> 00:00:56.851
Timestamps are valuable and limited.

12
00:00:56.851 --> 00:01:08.425
Creation, modification, access, metadata-change, and application-specific timestamps may mean different things depending on file system and operating system behavior.

13
00:01:08.425 --> 00:01:12.569
Copying a file can change some timestamps and preserve others.

14
00:01:12.569 --> 00:01:18.200
Moving a file within a volume may behave differently from copying it between volumes.

15
00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:23.517
Time zones, clock drift, and tool presentation can also affect interpretation.

16
00:01:23.517 --> 00:01:29.069
Deleted files and unallocated space can provide additional context at a high level.

17
00:01:29.069 --> 00:01:34.700
Unallocated space is space not currently assigned to active files by the file system.

18
00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:44.396
It may contain remnants, fragments, or nothing useful, depending on media behavior, wear leveling, file system activity, and acquisition scope.

19
00:01:44.396 --> 00:01:51.278
Analysts should avoid promising recovery or treating fragments as complete evidence without support.

20
00:01:51.278 --> 00:02:04.494
A practical content review often groups files by type and relevance: documents, spreadsheets, archives, scripts, executables, images, logs, configuration files, and unusual or unexpected items.

21
00:02:04.494 --> 00:02:15.598
It also looks for hidden directories, repeated naming patterns, staging folders, compressed collections, and files whose metadata conflicts with the visible story.

22
00:02:15.598 --> 00:02:19.899
Every finding should be tied back to the investigative question.
